explanations.E0062:
# E0062: optional used as if it were present

A field read, a method call, an index or a `for` needs a value, not an optional of one. An optional may be `None`, which has no fields, methods or elements.

## Why

Sisu has no null pointer errors at runtime: the checker makes the program say what happens when the optional is `None`. `?.` reads the member only when the optional holds a value, and gives `None` otherwise. In an assignment or a `push`, `?.` makes the change only when the optional holds a value, and does nothing otherwise. `if let` runs a block with the value only when it is present. `let ... else` and `?? return` unwrap the value and leave when it is `None`.

The checker first looks for the member on the value inside the optional, as `?.` does. When that value has no such member, as in `n.size()` on a `Node?` where `Node` has no `size`, or the member does not apply to it, the error is the one that `?.` gives, such as [E0063](E0063.md) or [E0058](E0058.md), not E0062: a `?.` would not fix it.

An assignment to an element, such as `m?.items[0] = 2`, writes into the array that `m?.items` holds. An `if let` of that array binds a copy, and you cannot write to a copy. The help names the object of the `?.` instead: `if let m = m { m.items[0] = 2 }` writes into the array of `m`.

A `push` onto an optional array, such as `xs.push(2)` on an `[i64]?`, gets no `if let` help, because an unwrapped array is a copy. The `?.` fix is the form that changes the array: `xs?.push(2)`. The help names the object of a `?.` only when that object stores the array, as in `bag?.items.push(4)`.

An optional of an optional, such as a `Node??`, has two layers, and `?.` takes only one off: `n?.value` on a `Node??` reads `value` from a `Node?`, which may be `None` too. Peel the other layer first with `??`: `(n ?? None)?.value`. A `.` on a `Node??` gets the same `?? None` help, with no fix, when it reads a field or calls a method. It gets the help too for a field assignment, a compound assignment and a `push` onto a field. An index, a slice, an element write and a `for` get the `if let` help in its place. So does an argument written with `mut`, or given to a `mut` parameter. The peeled form fails there. On an `[i64]??`, a `push` gets the `if let` help too, with no fix: the peeled `push` gives E0021. The help adds one `?? None` for each extra layer: on a `Node???`, it gives `((n ?? None) ?? None)?.value`.

## Broken

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    n.next.value
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    if let next = n.next {
        next.value
    } else {
        0
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Related

- [E0049](E0049.md): `?.` on a value that is not optional.

explanations.E0069:
# E0069: `sum` of an array that is not `[i64]` or `[f64]`

`sum()` adds the elements of an array of `i64` or of `f64`.

## Why

Only `i64` and `f64` elements have a sum of the same type. Sisu does not convert a `u8` or a `bool` to a number by itself.

The prelude writes `sum` in Sisu, only in an `extend [i64]` block and an `extend [f64]` block, so other arrays do not have it. The same error names any other method that the prelude gives to some array types but not to the type of your array.

The same error comes when you write the method as a function, as in `sum(bytes)` with a `[u8]`. The label says that `sum` is a method. When the array has the method, the error is [E0070](E0070.md) and its fix writes `bytes.sum()`.

## Broken

```sisu
fn main() {
    let flags = [true, false]
    print(flags.sum())
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
fn main() {
    let flags = [true, false]
    let mut count = 0
    for flag in flags {
        if flag {
            count += 1
        }
    }
    print(count)
}
```

explanations.E0155:
# E0155: `extend` of a type that is not a class or an enum

An `extend` block adds methods to a class or an enum. It cannot extend a built-in type, such as `i64` or `[i64]`, or a type parameter. A type alias is the type that it names, so `extend Grid` for `type Grid = [[char?]]` is an error that names `[[char?]]`, and `extend P` for `type P = Point` extends `Point`.

## Why

Only the prelude adds methods to a built-in type. A program that extends one could change what every other program means by `i64`. To add behavior to a number, write a function, or wrap the number in a class. A call of a method that the rejected block writes gets no [E0063](E0063.md), because the block is the cause. A type parameter stands for a different type at each use, so a block cannot add methods to it. Write an interface for what the type parameter must do, and use it as a bound.

A type parameter of the block must appear in its target, because a call finds the type of the parameter from the receiver. In `extend<T> Meters`, no receiver gives `T` a type. Remove the unused parameter.

A target can have type arguments: `extend<T> Stack<T>` adds methods for every `Stack`, `extend<T> Stack<[T]>` for every `Stack` of arrays, and `extend Stack<i64>` for one `Stack` only. The parameters of the block are the ones that its target names. In `extend<T> Stack<i64>`, the target names no `T`, and the error points at it. When the block uses `T` nowhere else, the fix removes the list of type parameters.

A block cannot name an associated type in its target or in the arguments of its interface, as `extend<S: Source> Wrap<S.Item>` or `extend<S: Source> Peek<S>: Convert<S.Item>`. A call finds the parameters of a block from the type of its receiver, and the receiver does not show which type a parameter stands for when it is written through an associated type. Write the target with a type parameter of its own, such as `extend<T> Wrap<T>`, and give that parameter the bounds that the methods need.

An `any` type is no class and no enum either. `extend any Shape { ... }` is this error: the compiler writes the conformance of `any Shape` to `Shape` and to the interfaces that `Shape` inherits, and a program cannot write or replace it.

## Broken

```sisu
extend i64 {
    fn double(self) -> i64 {
        self * 2
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Meters {
    value: i64
}

extend Meters {
    fn double(self) -> i64 {
        self.value * 2
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(Meters(value: 1).double())
}
```

## Related

- [E0147](E0147.md): an interface used as a type.

explanations.E0156:
# E0156: call or type that more than one conformance answers

A name can have two answers when two interfaces or two conformances give it. A type can conform to two interfaces that both require a method of one name, and a call of that method on a value of the type has two answers. A type written through a type parameter, as `S.Item`, has two answers when two bounds of the parameter declare `Item`, and so does `T.Item` in the body of a type alias when two interfaces declare `Item`. The compiler picks a call's answer when only one returns the type that the context expects. A method in the body of the class always wins over a method of a conformance block.

## Why

The type of the context decides, with `let n: string = c.name()`. A call with no expected type, such as `let n = c.name()`, does not decide. Write the type that you expect. If the two answers return the same type, no annotation can tell them apart: call the method through a function with one bound, such as `fn label_of<T: Label>(x: T)`.

A generic interface lets one type conform to it more than once, with different type arguments. With `extend Celsius: Convert<Fahrenheit>` and `extend Celsius: Convert<Kelvin>`, the call `c.convert()` has two answers. The error names each one with its type arguments. The result that the context expects picks one: a `let` with a type, such as `let f: Fahrenheit = c.convert()`, a parameter, such as `take_kelvin(c.convert())`, a result, or a field. When no context picks, the help shows the first result that only one answer has. When no result is the only one of its answer, there is no help.

A type written through a type parameter can have more than one answer too. In `fn show<S: Source & Feed>(x: S.Item)`, the type `S.Item` could come from `Source` or from `Feed`, which both declare `Item`. A function has no other way to write the type, so give the parameter one of the bounds, or give the associated types two names.

A method can return a type that names a type parameter of its own. The context cannot choose such a result, unless the call writes the type argument, as in `c.make<i64>(1)`.

A call on a value of a type parameter can have more than one answer too. In `fn show<T: Label & Code>(x: T)`, the call `x.name()` could come from `Label` or from `Code`. The expected type picks one when only one of them returns it, as above. When both return the same type, no annotation can tell them apart. Give the function one bound instead.

## Broken

```sisu
interface Label {
    fn name(self) -> string
}

interface Code {
    fn name(self) -> i64
}

class Circle {
    r: f64
}

extend Circle: Label {
    fn name(self) -> string {
        "circle"
    }
}

extend Circle: Code {
    fn name(self) -> i64 {
        7
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Circle(r: 1.0)
    let n = c.name()
    print(n)
    print(c.r)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
interface Label {
    fn name(self) -> string
}

interface Code {
    fn name(self) -> i64
}

class Circle {
    r: f64
}

extend Circle: Label {
    fn name(self) -> string {
        "circle"
    }
}

extend Circle: Code {
    fn name(self) -> i64 {
        7
    }
}

fn main() {
    let c = Circle(r: 1.0)
    let n: string = c.name()
    let k: i64 = c.name()
    print(n)
    print(k)
    print(c.r)
}
```

## Related

- [E0034](E0034.md): a name defined twice.
